# Disk images - too big to track. images/README.md records the sha256 and
# images/*.map records which sectors were recoverable.
images/*.img

# Downloaded vendor artifacts. sources/README.md is the tracked manifest.
sources/*.bin
sources/*.pdf
sources/*.tar.gz
sources/*.tgz
# Chip datasheets ARE tracked (owner directive) - sources/chips/ is NOT ignored.
!sources/chips/
!sources/chips/**
# Board photos: large, non-regenerable captures. Manifest in sources/README.md.
sources/photos/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MTD dumps: NOTHING is tracked.
#
# The earlier rule here was "unique to this unit -> tracked, re-obtainable ->
# ignored". That was wrong, and dangerously so: the partitions unique to this
# unit are exactly the ones carrying secrets. A verification pass found
#
#   mtd09 EEPROM  - a 2048-bit RSA PRIVATE KEY at 0xe004 (verified: p*q == n,
#                   e*d mod lambda(n) == 1). The device's own secret.
#   mtd11 config  - two root password hashes ($6$ SHA-512, $5$ SHA-256) plus
#                   the prior LAN topology, recoverable from unallocated blocks
#                   with plain `strings` despite the live tree holding only
#                   lost+found and a version file.
#   mtd04 chike   - a personal photograph: a selfie of two identifiable people,
#                   Samsung SM-G975F, 2019-06-07, shipped as NAND filler.
#
# and the old rule selected precisely those three for committing. Ignore the
# lot; images/mtd/*/README.md carries sizes and hashes, which is the useful part.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
images/mtd/**/*.img

# TFTP staging area: vendor firmware copies (hundreds of MB each) plus scratch
# partition dumps. All regenerable - sources/README.md has the URLs and hashes.
images/tftp/

# Unpacked partition contents. Same reasoning as the raw .img files above, and
# it already bit once: an `unpacked/` tree held mtd "config" (root password
# hashes) and mtd "chike" (a personal photograph of two identifiable people).
# Neither is ours to publish. README.md per dump dir carries the useful part.
images/**/unpacked/

# Credentials - never committed. Nothing in the repo reads these; they are for
# the human. Kept broad on purpose: a secrets file that slips into a public repo
# cannot be un-published.
secrets.yaml
secrets.yml
secrets/
*.secret
.env
.netrc

tmp/
__pycache__/
